\section{Writing formulas}{

\itemize{
\item With no formula, a helper draws from a plain distribution, e.g.
\code{sim_num(mean = 40, sd = 12)}.
\item With a formula, the formula sets each person's expected value, e.g.
\code{sim_num(~ 2 + 0.5*age + 0.3*treated, sd = 1)}. The coefficients are the
true effects.
\item Interactions: \code{age:treated} and \code{age*treated} both mean age times treated.
\item Categorical predictors: use a comparison, e.g. \code{0.4*(group == "test")}.
\item Any R function works: \code{log(x)}, \code{x^2}, \code{ifelse(x > 5, 1, 0)}.
\item For \code{sim_bin()} the formula is on the log-odds scale; for \code{sim_count()} it
is on the log scale.
\item \code{sd} is the random error around the formula and defaults to 1. Use
\code{sd = 0} for an exact calculation such as \code{sim_num(~ x + 2, sd = 0)}.
}
